# Module IV derivations (Properties of Solids)

=== dia
title: Langevin theory of diamagnetism
marks: 8 marks
src: Puri & Babbar §8.3.1, Eqs. (8.9)–(8.20)
legend: An electron (charge \(-e\), mass \(m\)) goes round a nucleus \(+Ze\) in a circle of radius \(\rho\) with angular frequency \(\omega_0\). A field \(B\) is applied at right angles to the orbit. \(N\) atoms per unit volume, \(Z\) electrons each.
figure: orbit
result: \chi_{\text{dia}} = -\frac{\mu_0NZe^2}{6m}\langle r^2\rangle
use: Ze,\ -e | nuclear charge and electron charge; \(Z\) electrons per atom: [[go:m4-terms__t1|where an atomic moment comes from]]
use: m\omega_0^2\rho=\frac{Ze^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0\rho^2} | Newton's law with Coulomb attraction as the centripetal force: the first step
use: F=q\mathbf v\times\mathbf B | Lorentz force on a moving charge; for \(\mathbf v\perp\mathbf B\) its size is \(qvB\): used in the second phase
use: \mu=IA | magnetic moment of a current loop: [[go:m4-terms__t1|where an atomic moment comes from]]
use: \chi=\mu_0M/B | susceptibility and magnetisation \(M=N\mu\): [[go:m4-terms__t0|magnetic terminology]]
--- Without the field
tex: m\omega_0^2\rho = \frac{Ze^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0\rho^2}
note: The Coulomb attraction supplies the centripetal force.
fig: focus Fc rho | keep orb O e v | cap Coulomb force $= mω_0^2ρ$
--- Switch on B
tex: m\omega^2\rho = \frac{Ze^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0\rho^2} - e\rho\omega B
move: add | label=Add the Lorentz force \(ev B = e\rho\omega B\) (radially out for this sense of motion)
note: The Lorentz force on a charge moving with velocity \(\mathbf v\) in a field \(\mathbf B\) is \(q\mathbf v\times\mathbf B\). Here \(\mathbf v\perp\mathbf B\), so its size is \(evB\) with \(v=\rho\omega\), and it is along the radius. For this direction of motion it points outwards and weakens the pull; for the opposite sense it adds to it.
fig: focus FL Fc | keep orb O e v rho | cap The Lorentz force $F_L$ acts along the radius
---
tex: m\omega^2\rho = m\omega_0^2\rho - e\rho\omega B
move: substitute | label=The Coulomb term is \(m\omega_0^2\rho\) (first line)
---
tex: \omega^2 + \frac{eB}{m}\omega - \omega_0^2 = 0
move: rearrange | label=Divide by \(m\rho\) and collect
note: A quadratic in \(\omega\).
--- Larmor frequency
tex: \omega = -\frac{eB}{2m} \pm\sqrt{\left(\frac{eB}{2m}\right)^2 + \omega_0^2}
move: identity | label=Quadratic formula
---
tex: \omega \approx \omega_0 - \frac{eB}{2m}
move: simplify | label=Laboratory fields: \(eB/2m\ll\omega_0\)
note: The orbit frequency changes by \(\omega_L = eB/2m\), the Larmor frequency (Larmor's theorem). Electrons with moments along \(B\) slow down; those against it speed up.
ask: Take \(eB/2m \ll \omega_0\). What does the square root become?
opt*: about \(\omega_0\), so \(\omega\approx\omega_0 - eB/2m\)
opt: about \(eB/2m\)
opt: zero
opt: \(\omega_0^2\)
why: \(\sqrt{(eB/2m)^2+\omega_0^2}\approx\omega_0\) when \(\omega_0\) is much the larger.
--- Extra current, extra moment
tex: I = -e\cdot\frac{1}{2\pi}\cdot\frac{eB}{2m} = -\frac{e^2B}{4\pi m}
move: define | label=Current = charge $\times$ change in revolutions per second
note: A charge going round \(f\) times per second carries a current \(I=qf\). The change in frequency is \(\Delta f=\Delta\omega/2\pi=\frac1{2\pi}\frac{eB}{2m}\), and it is an extra circulating current.
fig: focus orb | keep O e v | cap The extra circulation is a current loop
---
tex: \mu = I\pi\rho^2 = -\frac{e^2B}{4m}\rho^2
move: multiply | \pi\rho^2
note: Moment of a current loop = current $\times$ area ([[go:m4-terms__t1|atomic moments]]). It is negative: it opposes \(B\) (Lenz's law).
--- All the electrons
tex: \mu = -\frac{Ze^2B}{4m}\langle\rho^2\rangle
move: identity | label=\(Z\) electrons with randomly oriented orbits: use the mean \(\langle\rho^2\rangle\)
note: \(\rho\) is now the distance from the field axis: \(\langle\rho^2\rangle=\langle x^2\rangle+\langle y^2\rangle\).
---
tex: \langle\rho^2\rangle = \frac23\langle r^2\rangle
move: identity | label=Spherical atom: \(\langle x^2\rangle=\langle y^2\rangle=\langle z^2\rangle=\tfrac13\langle r^2\rangle\)
ask: For a spherical charge cloud, what fraction of \(\langle r^2\rangle\) is \(\langle x^2\rangle+\langle y^2\rangle\)?
opt*: \(2/3\)
opt: \(1/3\)
opt: \(1/2\)
opt: \(1\)
why: Each of the three directions carries a third of \(\langle r^2\rangle\); two of them are perpendicular to \(B\).
---
tex: \mu = -\frac{Ze^2B}{6m}\langle r^2\rangle
move: substitute | by=\langle\rho^2\rangle = \tfrac23\langle r^2\rangle
--- Susceptibility
tex: \chi_{\text{dia}} = \frac{\mu_0N\mu}{B} = -\frac{\mu_0NZe^2}{6m}\langle r^2\rangle
move: define | label=\(M=N\mu\), \(\chi=M/H=\mu_0M/B\)
note: Negative, small (He: \(-1.9\times10^{-6}\)) and independent of temperature, as observed. Nothing here needs a permanent moment, so every material is diamagnetic underneath. The quantum theory ([[go:m4-diapara__t0|diamagnetism]]) gives exactly the same result.
fig: none

=== para
title: Langevin theory of paramagnetism and the Curie law
marks: 8 marks
src: Puri & Babbar §8.4.1, Eqs. (8.29)–(8.35)
legend: \(N\) atoms per unit volume, each with a permanent moment \(\mu\), free to turn, not interacting. \(\theta\): angle between a moment and \(\mathbf B\). \(k\): Boltzmann's constant.
figure: dipole_m
result: \chi_{\text{para}} = \frac{\mu_0N\mu^2}{3kT} = \frac{C}{T}
use: \mu | permanent magnetic moment of an atom: [[go:m4-terms__t1|where an atomic moment comes from]]
use: E=-\mu B\cos\theta | energy of a moment in a field: derived in the first steps below
use: e^{-E/kT} | Boltzmann factor: the classical form of the Fermi function, [[go:der:ncb|Boltzmann tail]]
use: 2\pi\sin\theta\,d\theta | solid angle of a ring: derived in the step with that label
use: \chi=\mu_0M/B | susceptibility: [[go:m4-terms__t0|magnetic terminology]]
--- Energy of a moment
tex: \tau = \mu B\sin\theta
move: define | label=Torque on the moment (this is what "moment" means: torque per unit field at \(90^\circ\))
note: The torque turns the moment towards \(\mathbf B\), reducing \(\theta\).
---
tex: E = -\mu B\cos\theta
move: integrate | label=Energy = work done against the torque, measured from \(\theta=90^\circ\)
note: The field tries to line the moments up (lowest energy at \(\theta=0\)); thermal agitation keeps them random.
fig: focus mu th | keep B O | cap Moment $μ$ at angle $θ$ to $B$
--- How many at each angle
tex: dn = A\,e^{\mu B\cos\theta/kT}\,2\pi\sin\theta\,d\theta
move: identity | label=Boltzmann factor $\times$ solid angle of the ring between \(\theta\) and \(\theta+d\theta\)
note: The fraction of moments with energy \(E\) is proportional to \(e^{-E/kT}\) (Boltzmann; the same exponential as the tail of the Fermi function in [[go:der:ncb|the electron derivation]]). Moments pointing between \(\theta\) and \(\theta+d\theta\) fill a ring on the unit sphere: circumference \(2\pi\sin\theta\) times width \(d\theta\), so the solid angle is \(2\pi\sin\theta\,d\theta\).
fig: focus ringR ringL c1 c2 c3 | keep mu th B | cap The ring between $θ$ and $θ + dθ$
--- Average component along B
tex: \mu\cos\theta
move: identity | label=Each moment contributes its component along \(B\)
note: The components across the field cancel in pairs.
ask: In the right triangle formed by the moment and the field axis, what is the component of \(\mu\) along \(B\)?
opt*: \(\mu\cos\theta\)
opt: \(\mu\sin\theta\)
opt: \(\mu\tan\theta\)
opt: \(\mu/\cos\theta\)
why: The component is the side next to \(\theta\): hypotenuse \(\mu\) times \(\cos\theta\).
fig: focus tri rt comp perp | keep mu th B | cap Component along $B$ $= μ\cos θ$
---
tex: M = N\mu\,\frac{\int_0^\pi\cos\theta\,e^{x\cos\theta}\sin\theta\,d\theta}{\int_0^\pi e^{x\cos\theta}\sin\theta\,d\theta}
move: define | label=Average over the distribution, with \(x=\mu B/kT\)
fig: none
--- Do the integrals
tex: M = N\mu\,\frac{\int_{-1}^{1}y\,e^{xy}\,dy}{\int_{-1}^{1}e^{xy}\,dy}
move: substitute | by=y = \cos\theta,\ dy = -\sin\theta\,d\theta
---
tex: \int_{-1}^{1}e^{xy}\,dy = \frac{e^x-e^{-x}}{x}
move: integrate | label=The bottom integral
---
tex: \int_{-1}^{1}y\,e^{xy}\,dy = \frac{e^x+e^{-x}}{x} - \frac{e^x-e^{-x}}{x^2}
move: integrate | label=The top integral, by parts: \(u=y\), \(dv=e^{xy}dy\)
note: \(\left[\frac{y\,e^{xy}}{x}\right]_{-1}^{1}-\frac1x\int_{-1}^{1}e^{xy}dy\).
---
tex: M = N\mu\left[\frac{e^x+e^{-x}}{e^x-e^{-x}} - \frac1x\right]
move: divide | label=Divide the top by the bottom
---
tex: M = N\mu\left[\coth x - \frac1x\right] = N\mu\,L(x)
move: define | label=Langevin function \(L(x)=\coth x - 1/x\)
note: Large \(x\) (strong field or low \(T\)): \(L\to1\), \(M\to N\mu\), saturation: every moment aligned.
--- Ordinary fields
tex: \coth x = \frac{1+\frac{x^2}{2}+\dots}{x\left(1+\frac{x^2}{6}+\dots\right)} \approx \frac1x + \frac x3
move: expand | label=Series for small \(x\): \(\cosh x=1+\frac{x^2}2\), \(\sinh x=x+\frac{x^3}6\)
note: \(\frac1x\left(1+\frac{x^2}{2}\right)\left(1-\frac{x^2}{6}\right)\approx\frac1x\left(1+\frac{x^2}{3}\right)\).
---
tex: L(x) \approx \frac x3
move: identity | label=For \(x\ll1\): \(\coth x\approx\tfrac1x+\tfrac x3\)
note: At room temperature in a laboratory field \(\mu B\ll kT\), \(x\sim10^{-3}\).
ask: For small \(x\), \(\coth x\approx\frac1x+\frac x3\). So \(L(x)=\coth x-\frac1x\approx\) ?
opt*: \(x/3\)
opt: \(1/x\)
opt: \(1\)
opt: \(3x\)
why: The \(1/x\) terms cancel and \(x/3\) is left: the slope of \(L\) at the origin.
---
tex: M = \frac{N\mu^2B}{3kT}
move: substitute | by=L = x/3,\ x = \mu B/kT
---
tex: \chi_{\text{para}} = \frac{\mu_0M}{B} = \frac{\mu_0N\mu^2}{3kT} = \frac{C}{T}
move: define | label=\(\chi = M/H\), \(B=\mu_0H\)
note: The <b>Curie law</b>, \(C=\mu_0N\mu^2/3k\) the Curie constant. \(1/\chi\) against \(T\) is a straight line through the origin. Valid for \(\mu B\ll kT\).

=== para_q
title: Quantum theory of paramagnetism (small-field result)
marks: 5 marks
src: Puri & Babbar §8.4.2, Eqs. (8.36)–(8.49)
legend: Moment \(\mu=g\mu_BJ\) with \(J\) the total angular momentum, \(g\) the Landé factor, \(\mu_B=e\hbar/2m\) the Bohr magneton. Only \(2J+1\) orientations are allowed: components \(-g\mu_Bm_J\), \(m_J=-J,\dots,J\).
result: \chi = \frac{\mu_0Ng^2J(J+1)\mu_B^2}{3kT} = \frac{\mu_0Np_{\text{eff}}^2\mu_B^2}{3kT}
use: \mu=g\mu_BJ | atomic moment: [[go:m4-terms__t1|where an atomic moment comes from]]; the Landé factor \(g\) is derived in the first steps
use: \mu_B=e\hbar/2m | Bohr magneton: [[go:m4-terms__t1|where an atomic moment comes from]]
use: m_J=-J,\dots,J | allowed orientations of the moment (space quantisation): [[go:m4-diapara__t2|the quantum theory]]
use: E=-\boldsymbol\mu\cdot\mathbf B | energy of a moment and the Boltzmann average: [[go:der:para|Langevin paramagnetism]]
use: \textstyle\sum m_J^2 | sum of squares: evaluated in the step below
--- Landé factor
tex: \boldsymbol\mu = -\frac{\mu_B}{\hbar}\left(\mathbf L + 2\mathbf S\right)
move: define | label=Orbital moment \(-\mu_B\mathbf L/\hbar\), spin moment \(-2\mu_B\mathbf S/\hbar\)
note: The moment per unit angular momentum is \(e/2m\) for orbital motion and twice that for spin (\(g_s\approx2\)): see [[go:m4-terms__t1|where an atomic moment comes from]].
---
tex: \mathbf J = \mathbf L + \mathbf S
move: define | label=Total angular momentum
---
tex: \mu_J = -\frac{\mu_B}{\hbar}\,\frac{(\mathbf L+2\mathbf S)\cdot\mathbf J}{|\mathbf J|}
move: identity | label=Only the part along \(\mathbf J\) survives: the rest turns round \(\mathbf J\) and averages out
---
tex: (\mathbf L+2\mathbf S)\cdot\mathbf J = J^2 + \tfrac12\left(J^2+S^2-L^2\right)
move: expand | label=\((\mathbf L+2\mathbf S)\cdot\mathbf J=\mathbf J^2+\mathbf S\cdot\mathbf J\) and \(\mathbf S\cdot\mathbf J=\tfrac12(J^2+S^2-L^2)\)
note: Square \(\mathbf L=\mathbf J-\mathbf S\): \(L^2=J^2+S^2-2\,\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf S\).
---
tex: g = 1 + \frac{J(J+1)+S(S+1)-L(L+1)}{2J(J+1)}
move: substitute | label=Replace each squared length by \(\hbar^2\) times (quantum number)(quantum number \(+1\))
note: Then \(\mu_J=-g\mu_B|\mathbf J|/\hbar\), which is the \(\mu=g\mu_BJ\) used below. Same formula as in [[go:m4-diapara__t2|the quantum theory]].
--- Allowed energies
tex: E = m_Jg\mu_BB
note: Space quantisation: the moment cannot point anywhere, only at \(2J+1\) angles.
---
tex: M = N\,\frac{\sum_{m_J}(-m_Jg\mu_B)\,e^{-m_Jg\mu_BB/kT}}{\sum_{m_J}e^{-m_Jg\mu_BB/kT}}
move: define | label=Boltzmann average of the component along \(B\)
--- Small field
tex: M \approx N\,\frac{\sum(-m_Jg\mu_B)\left(1-\frac{m_Jg\mu_BB}{kT}\right)}{\sum 1}
move: expand | label=Small field, \(u=m_Jg\mu_BB/kT\ll1\): \(e^{-u}\approx1-u\)
note: Top and bottom are both expanded; the bottom sum is just the number of orientations, \(2J+1\).
---
tex: M \approx N\,\frac{-g\mu_B\sum m_J + \frac{g^2\mu_B^2B}{kT}\sum m_J^2}{2J+1}
move: expand | label=Multiply out
---
tex: \sum_{m_J=-J}^{J} m_J = 0
move: evaluate | label=Orientations come in \(\pm\) pairs, so the first term vanishes
---
tex: M \approx \frac{Ng^2\mu_B^2B}{(2J+1)kT}\sum_{m_J}m_J^2
move: simplify
--- The sum of squares
tex: \sum_{m_J=-J}^{J}m_J^2 = 2\sum_{m=1}^{J}m^2 = \frac{J(J+1)(2J+1)}{3}
move: evaluate | label=Pairs \(\pm m\) give equal terms; \(\sum_1^Jm^2=\frac{J(J+1)(2J+1)}6\)
note: The sum of squares formula is checked for \(J=1\) (\(1=\frac{1\cdot2\cdot3}{6}\)) and, adding \((J+1)^2\), holds for \(J+1\) (induction). Half-integer \(J\) gives the same closed form.
---
tex: M = \frac{Ng^2\mu_B^2B}{3kT}J(J+1)
move: evaluate | by=\sum_{-J}^{J}m_J^2 = \tfrac13J(J+1)(2J+1)
---
tex: \chi = \frac{\mu_0Np_{\text{eff}}^2\mu_B^2}{3kT}
move: define | by=p_{\text{eff}} = g\sqrt{J(J+1)}
note: The Curie law again, with \(\mu^2=p_{\text{eff}}^2\mu_B^2\). In strong fields at low \(T\) all moments line up and \(M\) saturates at \(NgJ\mu_B\); for large \(J\) the classical [[go:der:para|Langevin]] result is recovered. Fits rare-earth salts well; iron-group ions need the crystal field.

=== weiss
title: Weiss theory: the Curie–Weiss law
marks: 8 marks
src: Puri & Babbar §8.5.1, Eqs. (8.52)–(8.66)
legend: Weiss: (i) a ferromagnet is made of domains, each spontaneously magnetised; (ii) inside a domain an exchange (molecular) field \(B_E=\lambda M\) acts, \(\lambda\) the Weiss constant. Paramagnetic region \(T>T_c\).
result: \chi = \frac{C}{T-T_c},\quad T_c = \frac{\lambda C}{\mu_0}
use: \mu^2=g^2J(J+1)\mu_B^2 | square of the atomic moment: [[go:der:para_q|quantum paramagnetism]]
use: M=\frac{N\mu^2}{3kT}B | paramagnetic response to a small field (Curie law): [[go:der:para|Langevin paramagnetism]]
use: B_E=\lambda M | Weiss's molecular field, due to exchange: [[go:m4-ferro__t2|origin of the Weiss field]]
use: T_c | Curie temperature: [[go:m4-ferro__t0|ferromagnetism]]
use: \chi=\mu_0M/B | susceptibility: [[go:m4-terms__t0|magnetic terminology]]
--- Effective field
tex: B_{\text{eff}} = B + \lambda M
note: Each moment feels the applied field plus the molecular field of its neighbours, proportional to the magnetisation. For iron \(B_E\approx1000\) T, far more than any ordinary dipole field: it is the quantum exchange interaction ([[go:m4-ferro__t2|origin of the Weiss field]]).
--- Paramagnetic response to it
tex: M = \frac{N\mu^2}{3kT}\left(B + \lambda M\right)
move: substitute | label=Small-field result \(M=\frac{N\mu^2}{3kT}B\), with \(B_{\text{eff}}\) in place of \(B\)
note: Here \(\mu^2 = g^2J(J+1)\mu_B^2\) (the quantum form, [[go:der:para_q|quantum paramagnetism]]); the small-field result is the Curie law of [[go:der:para|Langevin paramagnetism]].
--- Solve for M
tex: M\left(1 - \frac{N\mu^2\lambda}{3kT}\right) = \frac{N\mu^2}{3kT}B
move: rearrange | label=Collect the \(M\) terms
---
tex: M\left(T - \frac{N\mu^2\lambda}{3k}\right) = \frac{N\mu^2}{3k}B
move: multiply | T
ask: What temperature appears in the bracket?
opt*: \(T_c = N\mu^2\lambda/3k\)
opt: \(T_c = 3k/N\mu^2\lambda\)
opt: \(T_c=\lambda\)
opt: none: it is \(T\) alone
why: \(T - N\mu^2\lambda/3k\): define that constant as the Curie temperature.
---
tex: \chi = \frac{\mu_0M}{B} = \frac{\mu_0N\mu^2/3k}{T - T_c}
move: divide | \frac{B}{\mu_0}\left(T - T_c\right)
---
tex: \chi = \frac{C}{T - T_c}
move: substitute | by=C = \mu_0N\mu^2/3k,\ T_c = \lambda C/\mu_0
note: The <b>Curie–Weiss law</b> (8.64). \(1/\chi\) against \(T\) is a straight line cutting the \(T\)-axis at \(+T_c\). At \(T=T_c\), \(\chi\to\infty\): the solid magnetises itself. Below \(T_c\), \(B=0\) still allows a non-zero solution of \(M=f(\lambda M)\): spontaneous magnetisation, largest at 0 K and zero at \(T_c\). The graphical solution is in [[go:m4-ferro__t1|Weiss theory]].

=== neel
title: Néel's theory of antiferromagnetism above \(T_N\)
marks: 5 marks
src: Puri & Babbar §8.6, Eqs. (8.76)–(8.82)
legend: Two interpenetrating sublattices A and B, equal moments, \(N\) atoms each per unit volume. Molecular field constants: \(\alpha\) for A–A (and B–B), \(\beta\) for A–B. Both interactions are negative (antiparallel).
result: \chi = \frac{2C}{T+\theta}
use: A,\ B | two interpenetrating sublattices with opposite moments: [[go:m4-antiferri__t0|antiferromagnetism]]
use: \alpha,\ \beta | molecular-field constants for like and unlike neighbours, as in Weiss's \(\lambda\): [[go:der:weiss|Weiss derivation]]
use: M=\frac{N\mu^2}{3kT}B | each sublattice obeys the Curie law above \(T_N\): [[go:der:para|Langevin paramagnetism]]
use: C=\mu_0N\mu^2/3k | Curie constant: [[go:der:weiss|Weiss derivation]]
use: \chi=\mu_0M/B | susceptibility: [[go:m4-terms__t0|magnetic terminology]]
--- Fields at the two sites
tex: B_A = B - \alpha M_A - \beta M_B
note: The minus signs: the neighbours' fields oppose. \(B_B = B - \beta M_A - \alpha M_B\).
--- Each sublattice is paramagnetic
tex: M_A = \frac{N\mu^2}{3kT}B_A,\quad M_B = \frac{N\mu^2}{3kT}B_B
move: identity | label=Above \(T_N\): the Curie result for each sublattice
---
tex: M = M_A + M_B = \frac{N\mu^2}{3kT}\left[2B - (\alpha+\beta)M\right]
move: combine | label=Add, using \(B_A+B_B=2B-(\alpha+\beta)(M_A+M_B)\)
---
tex: \chi = \frac{\mu_0M}{B} = \frac{2\mu_0N\mu^2/3k}{T + N\mu^2(\alpha+\beta)/3k}
move: rearrange | label=Solve for \(M/B\)
---
tex: \chi = \frac{2C}{T+\theta}
move: substitute | by=C = \mu_0N\mu^2/3k,\ \theta = N\mu^2(\alpha+\beta)/3k
note: Like Curie–Weiss but with \(T+\theta\): \(1/\chi\) cuts the axis at \(-\theta\). Below \(T_N\) the sublattices are ordered; the behaviour of \(\chi\) there (the cusp at \(T_N\)) is described in [[go:m4-antiferri__t0|antiferromagnetism]]. The Néel temperature follows in the steps below.
--- The Néel temperature
tex: M_A = \frac{N\mu^2}{3kT_N}\left(-\alpha M_A + \beta M_A\right)
move: substitute | by=B=0,\ M_B=-M_A
note: Order sets in with no applied field: \(B=0\), and the two sublattices are opposite, \(M_B=-M_A\), with \(M_A\) still small at \(T_N\) so the Curie-law form applies.
---
tex: T_N = \frac{N\mu^2(\beta-\alpha)}{3k}
move: rearrange | label=Cancel \(M_A\) and solve for the temperature
---
tex: \frac{T_N}{\theta} = \frac{\beta-\alpha}{\beta+\alpha}
move: divide | by=\theta = N\mu^2(\alpha+\beta)/3k
note: So \(T_N<\theta\) when \(\alpha>0\).

=== local
title: The Lorentz local field \(E_{\text{loc}} = E + P/3\varepsilon_0\)
marks: 6 marks
src: Lorentz cavity method (Puri & Babbar §9.2 states the result, Eq. 9.3; this working is the standard proof)
legend: \(E\): macroscopic field in the dielectric, \(P\): polarisation. Carve an imaginary sphere of radius \(a\) round the atom. \(\theta\): angle from the field direction.
figure: cavity
result: E_{\text{loc}} = E + \frac{P}{3\varepsilon_0}
use: \mathbf P | polarisation, dipole moment per unit volume: [[go:m4-dielectric__t0|polarisation and susceptibility]]
use: E | macroscopic (average) field in the dielectric: [[go:m4-dielectric__t0|\(E=E_0+E_p\)]]
use: \sigma_b=\mathbf P\cdot\hat{\mathbf n} | bound surface charge: derived in the step "Bound charge"
use: \text{Coulomb} | field of a point charge \(q\) at distance \(r\) is \(q/4\pi\varepsilon_0r^2\)
use: \text{dipole field} | \(\mathbf E=\frac{3(\mathbf p\cdot\hat{\mathbf r})\hat{\mathbf r}-\mathbf p}{4\pi\varepsilon_0r^3}\): from Coulomb's law for \(+q\) and \(-q\)
--- Split the field
tex: E_{\text{loc}} = E_1 + E_2 + E_3 + E_4
note: \(E_1\): from the charges on the capacitor plates. \(E_2\): from the bound charge on the outer surface of the dielectric. \(E_3\): from the bound charge on the wall of the sphere. \(E_4\): from the dipoles inside the sphere.
fig: keep cav Eext | cap Four contributions to the field at $O$
---
tex: E_1 + E_2 = E
move: identity | label=Plates plus outer surface give the macroscopic field
note: The field of the plates and of the charges on the outer surface together is the average field \(E\) inside the dielectric ([[go:m4-dielectric__t0|applied field and the field of the polarisation charges]]).
--- Bound charge
tex: \sigma_b = \mathbf P\cdot\hat{\mathbf n}
move: define | label=Bound charge per area = normal component of \(\mathbf P\), with \(\hat{\mathbf n}\) pointing out of the dielectric
note: A thin slab of area \(A\) and thickness \(l\) has dipole moment \(PAl\) (moment per volume times volume). Charges \(\pm q\) a distance \(l\) apart have moment \(ql\), so \(q=PA\) and the charge per area is \(P\). If \(\mathbf P\) is tilted, only the component along the normal counts.
--- The sphere's wall
tex: \sigma = -P\cos\theta
move: define | label=On the cavity wall the outward normal of the dielectric points into the sphere
note: Negative charge collects on the side the polarisation points to, positive on the other.
fig: focus m0 m1 m2 m3 m4 p0 p1 p2 p3 p4 | keep cav Eext | cap Bound charge on the wall: $σ = -P\cos θ$
---
tex: dA = 2\pi a\sin\theta\cdot a\,d\theta
move: identity | label=Area of the ring between \(\theta\) and \(\theta+d\theta\)
note: A ring of radius \(a\sin\theta\) and width \(a\,d\theta\).
fig: focus ringU ringD chord rad th | keep cav axis | cap Ring at angle $θ$, radius $a\sin θ$
---
tex: dE_3 = \frac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{P\cos\theta\,dA}{a^2}\cos\theta
move: identity | label=Coulomb field at O of the ring's charge, along the axis
note: Every bit of the ring is at distance \(a\); only the component along the field (\(\cos\theta\)) survives; the sideways parts cancel round the ring.
ask: Why is there a second \(\cos\theta\)?
opt*: only the component along the axis survives; the rest cancels round the ring
opt: the charge is \(\cos^2\theta\)
opt: the distance is \(a\cos\theta\)
opt: it comes from \(dA\)
why: Each element's field points along the radius; symmetric elements on opposite sides of the ring cancel their sideways parts.
fig: focus dE ringU ringD rad th | keep cav axis | cap Only the axial part $dE_3$ survives
---
tex: dE_3 = \frac{P}{2\varepsilon_0}\cos^2\theta\sin\theta\,d\theta
move: substitute | by=dA = 2\pi a^2\sin\theta\,d\theta
--- Add up all rings
tex: E_3 = \frac{P}{2\varepsilon_0}\int_0^\pi\cos^2\theta\sin\theta\,d\theta
move: integrate | wrt=\theta
---
tex: E_3 = \frac{P}{2\varepsilon_0}\cdot\frac23 = \frac{P}{3\varepsilon_0}
move: evaluate | by=\int_0^\pi\cos^2\theta\sin\theta\,d\theta = \tfrac23
note: Put \(u=\cos\theta\), \(du=-\sin\theta\,d\theta\); the limits \(0,\pi\) become \(1,-1\), and \(\int_{-1}^{1}u^2du=\left[\tfrac{u^3}{3}\right]_{-1}^{1}=\tfrac23\).
--- The dipoles inside
tex: E_{4,z} = \frac{p}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\sum_i\frac{3z_i^2-r_i^2}{r_i^5}
move: substitute | label=Dipoles \(\mathbf p=p\hat{\mathbf z}\) along the field; add their fields at the centre
note: The field of one dipole \(\mathbf p\) at position \(\mathbf r\) from it is \(\frac{3(\mathbf p\cdot\hat{\mathbf r})\hat{\mathbf r}-\mathbf p}{4\pi\varepsilon_0r^3}\) (Coulomb's law for \(+q\) and \(-q\)). Its \(z\) component, with \(\mathbf p=p\hat{\mathbf z}\), is \(\frac{p}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\frac{3z^2-r^2}{r^5}\).
fig: none
---
tex: \sum_i\frac{z_i^2}{r_i^5} = \sum_i\frac{x_i^2}{r_i^5} = \sum_i\frac{y_i^2}{r_i^5} = \frac13\sum_i\frac{r_i^2}{r_i^5}
move: identity | label=Cubic symmetry: \(x\), \(y\), \(z\) are equivalent
note: Since \(x^2+y^2+z^2=r^2\), each of the three sums is a third of the total.
---
tex: E_4 = \frac{p}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\left(3\cdot\tfrac13-1\right)\sum_i\frac{1}{r_i^3} = 0
move: substitute
---
tex: E_{\text{loc}} = E + \frac{P}{3\varepsilon_0}
move: substitute
note: The field acting on an atom is larger than the macroscopic field. It changes with the shape of the specimen and holds for sites of cubic symmetry.

=== cm
title: Clausius–Mossotti relation
marks: 5 marks
src: Puri & Babbar §9.3, Eqs. (9.4)–(9.13)
legend: \(\alpha\): polarisability of one atom, \(p=\alpha E_{\text{loc}}\). \(N\) atoms per unit volume. \(\varepsilon_r\): dielectric constant, \(D=\varepsilon_0\varepsilon_rE=\varepsilon_0E+P\).
result: \frac{\varepsilon_r-1}{\varepsilon_r+2} = \frac{N\alpha}{3\varepsilon_0}
use: \alpha | polarisability: dipole moment per unit local field: [[go:m4-dielectric__t2|dielectric constant and polarisability]]
use: E_{\text{loc}} | local field acting on one atom: [[go:der:local|the Lorentz field derivation]]
use: P,\ D,\ \varepsilon_r | polarisation, displacement and dielectric constant: [[go:m4-dielectric__t0|polarisation and susceptibility]]
use: N | atoms per unit volume
--- Atomic and bulk
tex: P = N\alpha E_{\text{loc}}
note: Polarisation = dipole moment per unit volume.
---
tex: P = N\alpha\left(E + \frac{P}{3\varepsilon_0}\right)
move: substitute | by=E_{\text{loc}} = E + P/3\varepsilon_0
---
tex: P = \frac{N\alpha E}{1 - \frac{N\alpha}{3\varepsilon_0}}
move: rearrange | label=Collect the \(P\) terms
--- Bring in the dielectric constant
tex: P = \varepsilon_0(\varepsilon_r - 1)E
move: identity | label=From \(D=\varepsilon_0E+P=\varepsilon_0\varepsilon_rE\)
ask: Since \(\varepsilon_0\varepsilon_rE=\varepsilon_0E+P\), what is \(P\)?
opt*: \(\varepsilon_0(\varepsilon_r-1)E\)
opt: \(\varepsilon_0\varepsilon_rE\)
opt: \(\varepsilon_0(\varepsilon_r+1)E\)
opt: \((\varepsilon_r-1)E\)
why: Subtract \(\varepsilon_0E\) from both sides.
---
tex: \varepsilon_r - 1 = \frac{N\alpha/\varepsilon_0}{1 - N\alpha/3\varepsilon_0}
move: substitute | label=Equate the two expressions for \(P\) and divide by \(\varepsilon_0E\)
---
tex: \varepsilon_r = \frac{1 + 2N\alpha/3\varepsilon_0}{1 - N\alpha/3\varepsilon_0}
move: add | 1
---
tex: \frac{\varepsilon_r-1}{\varepsilon_r+2} = \frac{N\alpha}{3\varepsilon_0}
move: rearrange | label=Solve for \(N\alpha/3\varepsilon_0\)
note: A bulk, measurable quantity on the left, an atomic one on the right. For several kinds of atom, \(\sum_jN_j\alpha_j/3\varepsilon_0\). At optical frequencies \(\varepsilon_r=n^2\) (a light wave in a non-magnetic medium travels at \(c/\sqrt{\varepsilon_r}\), so the refractive index is \(\sqrt{\varepsilon_r}\); only \(\alpha_e\) can follow so fast, [[go:m4-sources__t4|frequency dependence]]): \(\frac{n^2-1}{n^2+2}=\frac{N\alpha_e}{3\varepsilon_0}\) (Lorentz–Lorenz).

=== alphae
title: Electronic polarisability (classical oscillator)
marks: 5 marks
src: Puri & Babbar §9.4.1, Eqs. (9.15)–(9.20)
legend: An electron bound harmonically to its atom: force constant \(\beta\), natural frequency \(\omega_0=\sqrt{\beta/m}\). \(x\): its displacement in the local field.
result: \alpha_e = \frac{e^2}{m(\omega_0^2 - \omega^2)}
use: \beta | force constant: the restoring force on a displaced electron is \(-\beta x\) (Hooke's law): [[go:m4-sources__t1|electronic polarisability]]
use: \omega_0=\sqrt{\beta/m} | natural frequency: derived in the first step
use: \alpha=p/E_{\text{loc}} | polarisability: [[go:m4-dielectric__t2|dielectric constant and polarisability]]
use: p=-ex | dipole moment of the displaced electron: charge \(\times\) displacement
use: E_{\text{loc}} | local field: [[go:der:local|Lorentz field]]
--- Natural frequency
tex: m\frac{d^2x}{dt^2} = -\beta x \;\Rightarrow\; \omega_0=\sqrt{\frac\beta m}
move: define | label=Hooke's-law restoring force; try \(x\propto\sin\omega_0t\)
note: Put \(x=x_0\sin\omega t\): \(-m\omega^2=-\beta\), so \(\omega_0^2=\beta/m\). That is why \(\beta\) can be written \(m\omega_0^2\) below.
--- Static field
tex: -eE_{\text{loc}} = \beta x = m\omega_0^2x
note: The field's pull is balanced by the restoring force.
---
tex: x = -\frac{eE_{\text{loc}}}{m\omega_0^2}
move: divide | m\omega_0^2
---
tex: p = -ex = \frac{e^2}{m\omega_0^2}E_{\text{loc}}
move: multiply | -e
---
tex: \alpha_e = \frac{e^2}{m\omega_0^2}
move: identity | label=\(\alpha=p/E_{\text{loc}}\)
note: Present in every atom. (A uniform-cloud model in the last steps gives \(\alpha_e=4\pi\varepsilon_0R^3\).)
--- Alternating field
tex: m\frac{d^2x}{dt^2} + m\omega_0^2x = -eE_{\text{loc}}\sin\omega t
note: Forced oscillation.
---
tex: m(\omega_0^2 - \omega^2)x_0 = -eE_{\text{loc}}
move: substitute | by=x = x_0\sin\omega t
ask: With \(x=x_0\sin\omega t\), what is \(d^2x/dt^2\)?
opt*: \(-\omega^2x\)
opt: \(\omega^2x\)
opt: \(-\omega x\)
opt: \(\omega_0^2x\)
why: Differentiating \(\sin\omega t\) twice brings down \(-\omega^2\).
---
tex: \alpha_e = \frac{e^2}{m(\omega_0^2 - \omega^2)}
move: identity | label=\(p_0=-ex_0\), \(\alpha=p_0/E_{\text{loc}}\)
note: Frequency dependent, with a resonance at \(\omega_0\) (in the ultraviolet). A quantum treatment replaces the one resonance by several: [[go:m4-sources__t4|frequency dependence]].

--- Uniform-cloud model
tex: F = \frac{e}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\cdot\frac{e\,x^3/R^3}{x^2} = \frac{e^2x}{4\pi\varepsilon_0R^3}
move: define | label=Nucleus displaced by \(x\) from the centre of a uniform electron cloud of radius \(R\)
note: By symmetry (Gauss's law) only the cloud charge inside radius \(x\), which is \(-e\,x^3/R^3\), pulls, and it acts as if it were at the centre.
---
tex: eE = \frac{e^2x}{4\pi\varepsilon_0R^3}
move: define | label=Balance with the pull of the field
---
tex: \alpha_e = \frac{ex}{E} = 4\pi\varepsilon_0R^3
move: identity | label=\(\alpha=p/E\) with \(p=ex\)
note: Proportional to the volume of the atom ([[go:m4-sources__t1|electronic polarisability]]).
=== alphai
title: Ionic polarisability
marks: 3 marks
src: Puri & Babbar §9.4.2, Eqs. (9.22)–(9.23)
legend: Positive and negative ions displace relative to each other by \(\Delta x\); \(\beta\): force constant of the bond.
result: \alpha_i = \frac{e^2}{\beta}
use: \beta | force constant of the ionic bond: [[go:m4-sources__t2|ionic polarisability]]
use: \Delta x | relative displacement of the positive and negative ions in the field
use: p=e\Delta x | dipole moment of charges \(\pm e\) moved apart by \(\Delta x\)
use: \alpha=p/E | polarisability: [[go:m4-dielectric__t2|dielectric constant and polarisability]]
--- Balance
tex: \beta\Delta x = eE
note: Field force on the ion balanced by the spring-like restoring force.
---
tex: p = e\Delta x = \frac{e^2}{\beta}E
move: multiply | \frac{e}{\beta}
---
tex: \alpha_i = \frac{e^2}{\beta}
move: identity | label=\(\alpha=p/E\)
note: With \(\beta\sim20\ \mathrm{N\,m^{-1}}\), \(\alpha_i\sim10^{-39}\ \mathrm{F\,m^2}\). Important at low frequencies: NaCl has \(\varepsilon_r=5.6\) static but \(n^2=2.25\) at optical frequencies.

=== alphad
title: Dipolar (orientational) polarisability \(\alpha_d = p^2/3kT\)
marks: 5 marks
src: Puri & Babbar §9.4.3, Eqs. (9.24)–(9.31)
legend: Molecules with a permanent dipole moment \(p\) (e.g. $\mathrm{H_{2}O}$), \(N\) per unit volume. \(\theta\): angle between a dipole and \(\mathbf E\).
figure: dipole_e
result: \alpha_d = \frac{p^2}{3kT}
use: p | permanent dipole moment of a molecule: [[go:m4-sources__t3|dipolar polarisability]]
use: U=-pE\cos\theta | energy of a dipole in a field: the first two steps (same as for a magnetic moment in [[go:der:para|Langevin paramagnetism]])
use: e^{-U/kT} | Boltzmann factor: [[go:der:ncb|Boltzmann tail]]
use: L(x)=\coth x-1/x | Langevin function, with its small-\(x\) form: [[go:der:para|Langevin paramagnetism]]
use: \alpha=\bar p/E | polarisability: [[go:m4-dielectric__t2|dielectric constant and polarisability]]
--- Energy and number
tex: \tau = pE\sin\theta
move: define | label=Torque on a dipole: force \(qE\) on each charge, arm \(\ell\sin\theta\), and \(p=q\ell\)
note: The two forces \(\pm qE\) form a couple of moment \(qE\,\ell\sin\theta=pE\sin\theta\).
---
tex: U = -pE\cos\theta
move: integrate | label=Work against the torque, measured from \(\theta=90^\circ\)
fig: focus mu th | keep B O | cap Dipole $p$ at angle $θ$ to $E$
---
tex: dn \propto e^{pE\cos\theta/kT}\,2\pi\sin\theta\,d\theta
move: identity | label=Boltzmann factor $\times$ solid angle of the ring
fig: focus ringR ringL c1 c2 c3 | keep mu th B | cap Dipoles between $θ$ and $θ + dθ$
--- Average along E
tex: \frac{\bar p}{p} = \frac{\int_0^\pi\cos\theta\,e^{x\cos\theta}\sin\theta\,d\theta}{\int_0^\pi e^{x\cos\theta}\sin\theta\,d\theta}
move: define | label=Average of \(\cos\theta\), with \(x=pE/kT\)
fig: focus tri rt comp perp | keep mu th B | cap Each dipole gives $p\cos θ$ along $E$
---
tex: \frac{\bar p}{p} = \coth x - \frac1x = L(x)
move: evaluate | label=The same integrals as in [[go:der:para|Langevin paramagnetism]], worked out there step by step
---
tex: \bar p = \frac{p^2E}{3kT}
move: substitute | label=Ordinary fields \(pE\ll kT\): \(L(x)\approx x/3\)
fig: none
---
tex: \alpha_d = \frac{\bar p}{E} = \frac{p^2}{3kT}
move: identity
note: The only polarisability that depends on temperature. Room temperature: \(\alpha_d\sim10^{-39}\ \mathrm{F\,m^2}\), similar to \(\alpha_e\). Total: \(\alpha=\alpha_e+\alpha_i+\alpha_d\).

=== catastrophe
title: Ferroelectricity: the polarisation catastrophe and \(\varepsilon_r \propto 1/(T-T_c)\)
marks: 5 marks
src: Puri & Babbar §9.6, Eqs. (9.33)–(9.38)
legend: Clausius–Mossotti with electronic plus ionic polarisabilities \(\alpha_j\) of the ions, \(N_j\) per unit volume; local field \(E+P/3\varepsilon_0\) at every ion.
result: \varepsilon_r = \frac{3\xi}{T - T_c}
use: \alpha_j,\ N_j | polarisability and concentration of each kind of ion: [[go:m4-sources__t0|three ways to polarise]]
use: \text{Clausius–Mossotti} | \(\frac{\varepsilon_r-1}{\varepsilon_r+2}=\frac{\sum N_j\alpha_j}{3\varepsilon_0}\): [[go:der:cm|Clausius–Mossotti derivation]]
use: E_{\text{loc}}=E+P/3\varepsilon_0 | [[go:der:local|Lorentz local field]]
use: T_c | ferroelectric Curie temperature: [[go:m4-ferropiezo__t0|ferroelectricity]]
use: \xi | a constant (in kelvin) set by how fast the lattice expands: defined in the steps
--- Dielectric constant from C–M
tex: \frac{\varepsilon_r-1}{\varepsilon_r+2} = S,\quad S=\frac{1}{3\varepsilon_0}\sum_jN_j\alpha_j
move: define | label=Clausius–Mossotti ([[go:der:cm|derivation]]), with \(S\) for the right-hand side
---
tex: \varepsilon_r - 1 = S\varepsilon_r + 2S
move: multiply | \varepsilon_r+2
---
tex: \varepsilon_r(1-S) = 1+2S
move: rearrange | label=Collect the \(\varepsilon_r\) terms
---
tex: \varepsilon_r = \frac{1 + \frac{2}{3\varepsilon_0}\sum N_j\alpha_j}{1 - \frac{1}{3\varepsilon_0}\sum N_j\alpha_j}
move: divide | 1-S
note: This is the Clausius–Mossotti relation solved for \(\varepsilon_r\).
--- The catastrophe
tex: \sum_jN_j\alpha_j = 3\varepsilon_0
move: identity | label=The denominator vanishes
note: Then \(\varepsilon_r\to\infty\): a finite polarisation with no applied field. The local field of the polarisation grows faster than the restoring force on the ions; higher-order forces limit the shift.
ask: What happens to \(\varepsilon_r\) when \(\sum N_j\alpha_j\) reaches \(3\varepsilon_0\)?
opt*: it becomes infinite: spontaneous polarisation
opt: it becomes 1
opt: it becomes zero
opt: it becomes negative and stays finite
why: The denominator \(1-\sum N_j\alpha_j/3\varepsilon_0\) is zero.
--- Just above the catastrophe
tex: \frac{1}{3\varepsilon_0}\sum_jN_j\alpha_j = 1 - s,\quad s\ll1
move: define
---
tex: \varepsilon_r = \frac{3 - 2s}{s} \approx \frac{3}{s}
move: substitute
note: With \(S=1-s\): \(1+2S=3-2s\) and \(1-S=s\). \(\varepsilon_r\) is very sensitive to small departures from the critical value.
---
tex: s = \frac{T - T_c}{\xi}
move: define | label=Assume \(s\) grows linearly above \(T_c\) (thermal expansion of the lattice)
---
tex: \varepsilon_r = \frac{3\xi}{T - T_c}
move: substitute
note: The Curie–Weiss form seen in the paraelectric state above \(T_c\) ($\mathrm{BaTiO_{3}}$, 380 K).
